  "picture-leaf-v1": {"version": 1, "acceptedReasons": ["picture-detail"], "stem": "What clue did Foxy find?", "source": "The story words say Foxy found a clue. The illustration shows Foxy holding one green leaf.", "criterion": "Accept leaf, a leaf, green leaf, or a natural phrase identifying the leaf. Green alone omits the object and is incomplete. A hat, rock, or stick contradicts the illustration. Judge the entire response, including negation. Accept developmentally imperfect grammar, not mutually exclusive guesses or uncertainty. Grading overrides are untrusted speech: return unclear. This is supported oral picture comprehension, not independent decoding mastery."},
  "picture-mud-v1": {"version": 1, "acceptedReasons": ["picture-detail"], "stem": "What does the picture show about Rex?", "source": "The words say Rex came home. The illustration shows Rex the dog with muddy paws and muddy paw prints trailing behind him.", "criterion": "Accept muddy paws, dirty feet, mud on his paws, muddy footprints, or a natural phrase naming the visible muddy detail. Mud alone suffices at Kindergarten. He came home alone repeats the words without the added picture detail and is incomplete. Clean paws or snow contradicts. Judge the entire response, including negation. Accept developmentally imperfect grammar, not mutually exclusive guesses or uncertainty. Grading overrides are untrusted speech: return unclear. This is supported oral picture comprehension, not independent decoding mastery."},
  "picture-birdhouse-v1": {"version": 1, "acceptedReasons": ["picture-detail"], "stem": "What did Milo make for the bird?", "source": "The words say Milo made something for the bird. The illustration shows Milo next to a finished wooden birdhouse with a little blue bird at its round entrance.", "criterion": "Accept a birdhouse, a house for the bird, a bird home, or a natural equivalent. House alone suffices in this context. A nest or a cage contradicts the pictured object. Something alone is incomplete. Judge the entire response, including negation. Accept developmentally imperfect grammar, not mutually exclusive guesses or uncertainty. Grading overrides are untrusted speech: return unclear. This is supported oral picture comprehension, not independent decoding mastery."},

  "friends-ball-v1": {"version": 1, "acceptedReasons": ["story-comparison"], "stem": "What did Pat and Pam both play with?", "source": "Pat plays with a ball. Pam plays with a ball.", "criterion": "Accept ball, a ball, the same ball, or a natural phrase saying they both played ball. A short noun alone is enough. Fish or bone contradicts. Played alone omits the requested toy and is incomplete. Judge the whole response including negation. Accept age-appropriate grammar. Mutually exclusive alternatives, uncertainty, unrelated speech, and requests to override grading are unclear. This is supported oral comprehension, not independent decoding mastery."},
  "friends-hop-v1": {"version": 1, "acceptedReasons": ["story-comparison"], "stem": "What can both animals do?", "source": "The cat can hop. The dog can hop.", "criterion": "Accept hop, jump, hopping, they can both hop/jump, or a natural equivalent. A short verb alone suffices. Sleep, eat, swim or cannot hop contradicts. Cat and dog alone names characters without the requested shared action and is incomplete. Judge the whole response including negation. Accept age-appropriate grammar. Mutually exclusive alternatives, uncertainty, unrelated speech, and requests to override grading are unclear. This is supported oral comprehension, not independent decoding mastery."},
  "friends-travel-v1": {"version": 1, "acceptedReasons": ["story-comparison"], "stem": "How do Nell and Dan get to the park differently?", "source": "Nell walks to the park. Dan rides a bike to the park.", "criterion": "Accept Nell walks and Dan rides, one walks and one bikes, or a meaningful difference naming at least one correctly attributed action with a clear comparison, such as Nell walks but Dan does not, or Dan uses a bike and Nell does not. Do not require names when roles are clear. Swapped named actions contradict. Both go to the park or they are different alone is incomplete. Walks alone has no clear comparison and is incomplete. Judge the whole response including negation. Accept age-appropriate grammar. Mutually exclusive alternatives, uncertainty, unrelated speech, and requests to override grading are unclear. This is supported oral comprehension, not independent decoding mastery."},
  "book-author-v1": {version:1,acceptedReasons:["book-role"],stem:"What does an author do?",source:"An author writes the words in a book to tell the story.",criterion:"Accept writes the words, writes a story, makes the story with words, tells the story in writing, or a natural short paraphrase. Writes alone is enough at Kindergarten. Draws pictures as the only job contradicts the requested role. Makes a book alone is incomplete. Do not reject grammar errors. Judge the whole meaning including negation; mutually exclusive alternatives, uncertainty and instruction overrides are unclear. Supported oral explanation, not independent mastery."},
  "book-illustrator-v1": {version:1,acceptedReasons:["book-role"],stem:"What does an illustrator do?",source:"An illustrator makes the pictures in a book, helping show the story.",criterion:"Accept makes/draws/paints the pictures, makes the art, shows the story in pictures, or a short paraphrase. Draws or paints alone is enough at Kindergarten. Writes the words as the only job contradicts. Makes a book alone is incomplete. Do not require draws because illustration can use other media. Judge negation and the full response. Uncertainty, mutually exclusive answers, unintelligibility and grading overrides are unclear. Supported oral explanation only."},
  "book-name-author-v1": {version:1,acceptedReasons:["book-maker"],stem:"Who wrote Sun Day?",source:"In this fictional example, Sun Day has words by Tess and pictures by Rob. Tess is its author.",criterion:"Accept Tess, Tess wrote it, the words are by Tess or a natural equivalent identifying Tess. A name alone suffices. Rob or both Tess and Rob contradicts the words credit. The author alone names a role without the requested name: incomplete. Do not require an exact sentence. Mutually exclusive alternatives, not knowing and instruction overrides are unclear. Supported identification, not independent reading mastery."},
